{"category": "other-info-disclosure", "code_owners": ["Vitor Avila <96086495+Vitor-Avila@users.noreply.github.com>", "Mike Bridge <michael.bridge@preset.io>", "Bikash <bikashprasadbarnwal@gmail.com>"], "code_scan_run_id": "csrun-7f80d40c5f5546f0b1d3b9d6690cd01a", "created_at": 1788537797, "description": "##### Overview

The `GET /api/v1/database/<pk>/related_objects/` endpoint returns the SQL Lab tab states associated with a database to any user who can read that database. Unlike the sibling `charts` and `dashboards` lists in the same response — which are each filtered with per-object `security_manager.can_access_*` checks — the `sqllab_tab_states` list is returned unfiltered. `DatabaseDAO.get_related_objects` selects every `TabState` row for the database regardless of its owning `user_id`, so a user leaks other users' SQL Lab tab labels, ids and active flags. Tab labels are user-authored free text and can carry sensitive context (project names, ticket numbers, etc.).

##### Attack Path

```
authenticated user with DB read --GET /database/<pk>/related_objects/--> DatabaseDAO.get_related_objects(pk) --query TabState by database_id only (no user_id filter)--> response.sqllab_tab_states --> other users' tab labels/ids disclosed
```

The endpoint is `@protect()`-gated so the caller must hold the Database `can_read` permission, but that permission does not imply authorization to see other users' private SQL Lab tabs. In `get_related_objects`, `charts` and `dashboards` are post-filtered by `can_access_chart`/`can_access_dashboard`, but `sqllab_tab_states` is built directly from `db.session.query(TabState).filter(TabState.database_id == database_id).all()`, with no filter on `TabState.user_id`. The response then returns all of them verbatim.

##### Preconditions

- Attacker has an authenticated Superset account with `can_read` on the target `Database` (e.g. any Alpha user, or a Gamma user granted database access).
- Other users have created SQL Lab tab states against that same database.
- No additional privilege is required; the leaked data is limited to tab id, label and active flag (not query SQL or results).", "finding_id": "sfind-818861306fb143168b88fd6eb824dc7d", "note": "Verified in source: DatabaseDAO.get_related_objects (daos/database.py:229-230) queries TabState by database_id only (no user_id filter); the endpoint (databases/api.py:1386-1389) returns tab states verbatim while charts/dashboards in the same response are can_access_chart/dashboard-filtered. Confirmed cross-user disclosure of other users' SQL Lab tab id/label/active to any principal with Database can_read, via a single authenticated GET. Leaked data is low-sensitivity metadata (labels can carry project/ticket context); no SQL or results exposed. Principal: authenticated user with Database can_read. No CODEOWNERS entry for this path. No realized attack chain.", "orchestrator_session_id": "93f3258cb7ab46a58fcfa174b7398c0a", "pr_url": null, "recommendation": "Scope the tab-state query to the requesting user (e.g. filter `TabState.user_id == get_user_id()` in `DatabaseDAO.get_related_objects`, or post-filter the list in the endpoint), consistent with how `charts` and `dashboards` are access-filtered in the same response. If the intent is only to warn about deletion impact, return a count of affected tab states rather than their labels.", "reference_snippets": [{"code": "        data = DatabaseDAO.get_related_objects(pk)
