{"category": "other-info-disclosure", "code_owners": ["MsfPablo <129399053+MsfPablo@users.noreply.github.com>", "Enzo Martellucci <52219496+EnxDev@users.noreply.github.com>", "Mehmet Salih Yavuz <salih.yavuz@proton.me>"], "code_scan_run_id": "csrun-31cb909296c94b0689f93176a9d2a8bd", "created_at": 1788623919, "description": "##### Overview

The dataset list (`GET /api/v1/dataset/`) and dataset show (`GET /api/v1/dataset/<pk>`) endpoints annotate each dataset with its attached Row Level Security (RLS) filters. When the caller lacks `can_read` on `RowLevelSecurity`, the sensitive fields (`clause`, `roles`) are redacted, but the RLS filter `id`, `name`, `filter_type` and `group_key` are still returned to any user who can read the dataset. This exposes the structure/naming of the deployment's row-level security policies (e.g. filter names like `tenant_acme_only`, group keys like `tenant`) to users who have no RowLevelSecurity permission. Impact is limited to metadata disclosure — the actual filter SQL clause and role bindings remain protected — and the redaction is clearly deliberate, so this may be an intentional design tradeoff to power the dataset UI.

##### Attack Path

```
authenticated user w/ dataset read --GET /api/v1/dataset/ or /<pk>--> handler annotates rls_filters --redacts clause/roles only--> response leaks RLS filter id/name/filter_type/group_key
```

Any authenticated user who can see a dataset receives that dataset's RLS filter summaries. The `can_read`/`RowLevelSecurity` gate at lines 437-439 and 1761 only decides whether the full detail (including `clause` and `roles`) vs. the reduced summary is returned — in both branches the filter names and group keys are disclosed. In `get_rls_filters_for_datasets` (DAO), the non-privileged summary and the privileged object contain the same name/filter_type/group_key fields, so for the list endpoint there is effectively no redaction of names. An attacker with a low-privilege account that can browse datasets can enumerate the naming and grouping of all RLS policies, which may reveal tenant identifiers or security-policy structure useful for further targeting.

##### Preconditions

- Attacker holds an authenticated Superset account that can read at least one dataset (dataset read is broadly granted, e.g. to Gamma-style roles).
- The dataset has one or more RLS filters attached (directly, or inherited via physical tables for virtual datasets).
- Attacker does NOT need `can_read` on `RowLevelSecurity`; the names/group keys are returned regardless.", "finding_id": "sfind-2548d37fa2cc40569f1868a95f620861", "note": "Triage: P2. Exploitability trivial (any authenticated user with dataset read), but impact low: only RLS filter id/name/filter_type/group_key leak; the sensitive clause + roles are redacted. Possibly an intentional UI tradeoff (redaction is deliberate) — kept as P2 rather than dismissed because names/group keys can reveal tenant/policy structure across a permission boundary. Same reduced-vs-full RLS branch pattern also appears in superset/commands/explore/get.py (lines 164-182) but that is a different endpoint, not a duplicate. No CODEOWNERS entry; owners are recent committers. Step 4: no attack chain realized.", "orchestrator_session_id": "e1e76d75589746cd9e8cacc1672b09dc", "pr_url": null, "recommendation": "If RLS filter names/group keys are considered sensitive, gate the entire `rls_filters` annotation behind `can_access("can_read", "RowLevelSecurity")` and omit the key entirely (or return only a boolean "has RLS" indicator) for users without that permission, rather than returning identifying metadata. If the exposure is intentional for the UI, document it explicitly and confirm filter names are not expected to carry sensitive tenant information.", "reference_snippets": [{"code": "                    rls_map = DatasetDAO.get_rls_filters_for_datasets(dataset_ids)
